WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:02.734
-I love that you're here
on Halloween, for many reasons.

00:00:02.734 --> 00:00:03.968
Just,
I'm just happy you're here.

00:00:03.968 --> 00:00:07.534
But also, you must run into
versions of you...

00:00:07.534 --> 00:00:08.601
-Yeah.
-...a lot...

00:00:08.601 --> 00:00:09.601
[ Laughter ]
-Sure. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:09.601 --> 00:00:11.567
Absolutely.
-...on Halloween.

00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:15.467
How does one react when --
-I mean, just --

00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:17.801
You know,
give a positive review.

00:00:17.801 --> 00:00:19.501
Don't -- you know.
-Yeah, I'm hoping, yes.

00:00:19.501 --> 00:00:21.100
-Yeah, no.
I'm not gonna, like,

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.133
give them notes
or critique them in any way.

00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:26.767
Yeah, and it is, like --
it's -- it's a lovely thing.

00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:28.968
My friends send me photos
of their friends' kids

00:00:28.968 --> 00:00:30.467
or their kids, like,
dressed up as Potter.

00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:32.567
And, yeah, it's awesome.
-When you --

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:36.067
When you were a child,
doing Harry,

00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:39.133
and seeing other kids as Harry,
was that odd -- more --

00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:40.734
Was that weird?
-I guess.

00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:42.834
Like -- but I think I was
probably saved from that lot by,

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:47.133
like, how sort of tragically
non-existent Halloween was

00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:48.567
in England growing up
when I was there.

00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:49.601
[ Laughter ]

00:00:49.601 --> 00:00:50.801
-It wasn't even a big deal,
right?

00:00:50.801 --> 00:00:52.100
-Not really.
Like, I've never been

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.601
trick-or-treating in my life,
which people regard

00:00:53.601 --> 00:00:55.467
as I've been neglected,
but I'm fine.

00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:57.167
[ Laughter ]

00:00:57.167 --> 00:00:58.367
And, yeah.
And so, I don't think --

00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:00.267
Yeah, it's never been, like,
as much of a --

00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:01.701
of a thing for me.
But now --

00:01:01.701 --> 00:01:02.801
now, lots of Harry's
running around, yeah.

00:01:02.801 --> 00:01:03.934
-Yeah, it's good.

00:01:03.934 --> 00:01:05.868
You're in the middle
of rehearsals right now.

00:01:05.868 --> 00:01:07.534
You returning to the theater.
-Yeah.

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:09.033
-We're very excited about this.
-Thank you. Yes.

00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:10.267
[ Cheers and applause ]
I'm terrified.

00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:12.434
-We love you. You're doing
all the cool things.

00:01:12.434 --> 00:01:13.801
-Thank you.
-I love these choices.

00:01:13.801 --> 00:01:16.300
What is this you're doing?
-Yes, it's a musical called

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.267
"Merrily We Roll Along,"
which some will know

00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:19.567
and others may not.
[ Cheers ]

00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:22.133
It's a Sondheim show.
It's one of --

00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:24.334
I've seen this production
in London many years ago,

00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:26.534
and it's one of the best things
I've ever seen.

00:01:26.534 --> 00:01:28.467
So now I get to be a part
of doing it here,

00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:31.067
which is very intimidating
but great.

00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:33.000
-Stephen Sondheim.
Yeah, this is a --

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.234
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:35.234 --> 00:01:38.234
-And I read about this.
They -- They revived it in --

00:01:38.234 --> 00:01:39.934
in London and won all these
awards and all this stuff.

00:01:39.934 --> 00:01:41.000
-Yeah.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.300
-So now you're bringing it
back here.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.667
-So, we're bringing it here
with the same director

00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:45.133
who did it in London,
Maria Friedman,

00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:46.234
who's just incredible.

00:01:46.234 --> 00:01:48.434
And the show is -- is amazing.

00:01:48.434 --> 00:01:50.033
It's incredibly complicated.

00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:52.200
And, you know,
I've done one musical before,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.067
but there were a lot of less,
like, five-part harmonies for me

00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:56.734
to be involved in in that.
But, yeah, I'm learning.

00:01:56.734 --> 00:01:57.834
Yeah.
-That's awesome. You're --

00:01:57.834 --> 00:01:59.334
you're co-starring
with Jonathan Groff.

00:01:59.334 --> 00:02:00.567
-Yeah, Jonathan Groff
and Lindsay Mendez.

00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:01.968
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yeah.

00:02:01.968 --> 00:02:06.667
Who I -- I could not wish
for a better two people.

00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:09.100
And, like, professionals
and talented people

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.334
but also human beings
to do this with.

00:02:11.334 --> 00:02:12.667
So I'm very, very lucky
to be working with them.

00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:14.567
-Yeah, good man.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:17.033
Previews -- Previews begin
November 21st.

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:18.534
-Oh, my God!
-It opens December 12th.

00:02:18.534 --> 00:02:19.901
Oh, he's like -- yeah.
-That's terrifying.

00:02:19.901 --> 00:02:21.567
-Yeah, don't leave right now.
Stay here.

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:24.767
-And it opens December 12th
at New York Theatre Workshop.

00:02:24.767 --> 00:02:27.501
Everyone should check that out.
Let's talk about --

00:02:27.501 --> 00:02:28.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:28.701 --> 00:02:31.868
Let's talk about
"Weird: The Al Yankovic Story."

00:02:31.868 --> 00:02:34.100
Finally, we can talk about this.
-Yes!

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:35.300
-It's out on Friday.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.000
-Yeah, I'm so excited
for people to see it.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.234
-The reviews are amazing.

00:02:38.234 --> 00:02:40.234
Congrats on all
the great buzz around this.

00:02:40.234 --> 00:02:42.434
-I know. Thank you.
-You play Al.

00:02:42.434 --> 00:02:45.167
-You play Weird Al.
-Yeah, I know.

00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:48.200
-When you first heard
that Weird Al wanted you

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.234
for this role,
what went through your head?

00:02:50.234 --> 00:02:53.133
-I mean, I knew Al --

00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:54.434
Not personally,
but I knew his work.

00:02:54.434 --> 00:02:56.067
I knew who he was.
So, when I got the e-mail

00:02:56.067 --> 00:02:57.367
being like,
"You've been offered the part

00:02:57.367 --> 00:02:59.167
of Al Yankovic in
'The Al Yankovic Story,'"

00:02:59.167 --> 00:03:01.801
I was like, "I mean,
I'm very flattered,

00:03:01.801 --> 00:03:04.667
but there are, surely,
other people that are closer,

00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:06.767
physically, to Al or to --"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:06.767 --> 00:03:07.934
You know, and sort of had
all that reaction.

00:03:07.934 --> 00:03:09.601
-You look nothing like Weird Al.
-Right!

00:03:09.601 --> 00:03:10.667
-At all.
-And, you know,

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:12.701
there's only about
4 foot between us.

00:03:12.701 --> 00:03:14.167
[ Laughter ]
And so, like --

00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:16.534
But then,
as soon as I was reading --

00:03:16.534 --> 00:03:18.667
Honestly, as soon as I opened
the first page of the script,

00:03:18.667 --> 00:03:21.100
I was like, "Oh, right. Okay.
I get what the joke is.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.334
Accuracy is not the point here.

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:24.133
We're doing something else
entirely."

00:03:24.133 --> 00:03:25.934
[ Laughter ]
And at -- at that point,

00:03:25.934 --> 00:03:27.701
I was like, "Oh, I can do
this version of Al."

00:03:27.701 --> 00:03:31.200
And it was -- it was so evident
from just reading the script

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.400
what a great, fun thing
this would be to do.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.601
It's, like, six songs
and two action sequences

00:03:37.601 --> 00:03:39.467
and, like, insane scenes
that you get to do.

00:03:39.467 --> 00:03:42.868
Yes, so it was a very easy yes.
-Yeah.

00:03:42.868 --> 00:03:45.734
What was it like --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:45.734 --> 00:03:47.234
Wait till you see it.
It's so fun.

00:03:47.234 --> 00:03:50.934
What's it like playing Weird Al
in front of Al?

00:03:50.934 --> 00:03:52.234
'Cause I'm assuming
he was on set?

00:03:52.234 --> 00:03:54.100
-Yes, he was.
He was there every day.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.868
I think it was, honestly,
stranger for him than me

00:03:57.868 --> 00:03:59.968
just because we are acting out
this, like,

00:03:59.968 --> 00:04:03.667
Bizarro World version
of parts of his life.

00:04:03.667 --> 00:04:05.267
And, also,
a weird thing happened.

00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:06.367
It's impossible.

00:04:06.367 --> 00:04:07.601
I use the word "weird"
so much anyway.

00:04:07.601 --> 00:04:09.067
-I know.
I thought about that.

00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:10.801
I thought about that
when talking about this.

00:04:10.801 --> 00:04:12.934
I'm like, "Is it weird
to say 'weird' in Weird Al?"

00:04:12.934 --> 00:04:14.167
-No, it's not.
-I'm like --

00:04:14.167 --> 00:04:16.033
-It's our only option.
-Yes, it is!

00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:18.267
We need another option
for the word "weird."

00:04:18.267 --> 00:04:20.734
-But so, we -- I --
The thing happen on film sets,

00:04:20.734 --> 00:04:22.367
which you know
where you get called

00:04:22.367 --> 00:04:25.334
by your character name a lot.
So, on set, I was referred to

00:04:25.334 --> 00:04:27.501
over the walkie-talkie and stuff
as "Weird Al."

00:04:27.501 --> 00:04:29.234
And then,
they needed something else

00:04:29.234 --> 00:04:34.734
to call Weird Al, so he ended up
being called "Real Al,"

00:04:34.734 --> 00:04:38.667
which after having crafted
a persona for like 40 years,

00:04:38.667 --> 00:04:40.934
it's probably really annoying
to be stripped of it

00:04:40.934 --> 00:04:42.267
on your first day on a film set.
-Yes!

00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:44.400
You're like, "I worked
my whole life to be Weird Al,

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.000
and now I'm Real --
Real Al Yankovic?"

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:48.334
-I'm Weird Al now.
Yeah.

00:04:48.334 --> 00:04:49.834
-Now you're Weird Al
and he's Real Al.

00:04:49.834 --> 00:04:52.300
Did he ever pull you aside and
go, "I wouldn't do it that way.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:56.400
I acted this way"?
-As you know, the version of Al

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:59.033
that I'm playing in the film
is so --

00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:01.701
It's, at times, like the real Al
but it is, at times,

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:03.701
so far from that, that that
wasn't really a concern.

00:05:03.701 --> 00:05:06.367
His main -- the thing he cared
about the most, I think,

00:05:06.367 --> 00:05:09.100
even in an insane version
of a biopic,

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.767
was that he not look terrible
at playing the instrument

00:05:11.767 --> 00:05:13.200
that he is famous for playing.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.701
And so most of his notes
were accordion-based.

00:05:15.701 --> 00:05:17.667
But most of it was,
particularly, about how much

00:05:17.667 --> 00:05:19.667
I am pumping the bellows.

00:05:19.667 --> 00:05:21.534
I was not pumping enough.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.534 --> 00:05:23.534
And so, I -- many of the notes
were just him being --

00:05:23.534 --> 00:05:24.801
'Cause, you know,
which is fair enough.

00:05:24.801 --> 00:05:27.334
You don't want to look crap
at playing the thing

00:05:27.334 --> 00:05:29.467
you're famous for being good at.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:29.467 --> 00:05:31.434
And, actually, it's not you
singing in the movie.

00:05:31.434 --> 00:05:33.334
It's Al's voice.
-Yes, absolutely. Yeah.

00:05:33.334 --> 00:05:35.133
-That was a choice
that you made. You go --

00:05:35.133 --> 00:05:37.567
-Yeah, we wanted --
one of the many sort of

00:05:37.567 --> 00:05:40.667
musical biopic tropes we wanted
to take the piss out of

00:05:40.667 --> 00:05:42.701
was the --
clearly a different voice

00:05:42.701 --> 00:05:44.501
coming out of the actor's head.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.501 --> 00:05:46.667
So that's why -- that's what --
I sang along on set,

00:05:46.667 --> 00:05:47.767
but, no, it's all Al
and everything.

00:05:47.767 --> 00:05:49.167
-Evan Rachel Wood
plays Madonna in this.

00:05:49.167 --> 00:05:51.133
-Right.
-By the way, she's phenomenal.

00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:52.834
-Spectacular in this film.
-Like, phenomenal.

00:05:52.834 --> 00:05:56.334
She's so funny.
And she can sing in real life.

00:05:56.334 --> 00:05:58.367
-Yes, she can.
-She sang on our show.

00:05:58.367 --> 00:06:01.267
-Yes, I know.
Yeah, well, so --

00:06:01.267 --> 00:06:03.667
The day after
we finished shooting,

00:06:03.667 --> 00:06:06.934
we had, like, a very small
wrap party at a karaoke place.

00:06:06.934 --> 00:06:10.067
And Evan got up and sang,
and me and the director

00:06:10.067 --> 00:06:11.934
were just
looking at each other like,

00:06:11.934 --> 00:06:13.400
"Why haven't we got her
to sing in the movie?

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.734
This is -- This is the stupidest
thing that we're --"

00:06:15.734 --> 00:06:18.033
'Cause she is --
I mean, I can, like, sing.

00:06:18.033 --> 00:06:19.567
She can sing.
-Sing.

00:06:19.567 --> 00:06:20.767
She's fantastic.
-Yeah.

00:06:20.767 --> 00:06:23.400
Her doing karaoke is truly
a thing to behold.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.300
-Before we show a clip,
is there anything

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:28.234
you want to say
to set up the movie?

00:06:28.234 --> 00:06:32.334
-Yeah, just that it's
the 100% historically accurate

00:06:32.334 --> 00:06:33.734
and unassailably true story

00:06:33.734 --> 00:06:35.667
of the life
of Weird Al Yankovic.

00:06:35.667 --> 00:06:38.667
Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:38.667 --> 00:06:40.501
-Wow.

00:06:40.501 --> 00:06:42.033
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:42.033 --> 00:06:45.334
Here's Daniel Radcliffe in
"Weird: The Al Yankovic Story."

00:06:45.334 --> 00:06:46.634
Take a look at this.

00:06:46.634 --> 00:06:49.767
-Whoo!
♪ My, my, my Sharona ♪

00:06:49.767 --> 00:06:53.167
Whoo!
-♪ My, my, my bologna ♪

00:06:53.167 --> 00:06:56.534
-Whoo!
-♪ My, my, my bologna ♪

00:06:56.534 --> 00:06:59.067
♪♪

00:06:59.067 --> 00:07:00.467
-Al?

00:07:00.467 --> 00:07:03.167
[ Suspenseful music ]
-♪ My, my, my bologna ♪

00:07:03.167 --> 00:07:06.100
♪♪

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:10.567
♪ My, my, my bologna ♪

00:07:10.567 --> 00:07:12.167
-You okay?

00:07:12.167 --> 00:07:20.901
♪♪

00:07:20.901 --> 00:07:22.701
♪ Ooh, my little hungry one ♪

00:07:22.701 --> 00:07:23.834
♪ Hungry one ♪

00:07:23.834 --> 00:07:27.834
♪ Open up a package
of my bologna ♪

00:07:27.834 --> 00:07:29.334
-Oh!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:29.334 --> 00:07:31.667
A legend is born!

00:07:31.667 --> 00:07:34.000
Daniel Radcliffe, everyone!

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:37.334
"Weird: The Al Yankovic Story"
is available for free,

00:07:37.334 --> 00:07:40.934
exclusively on the Roku Channel
this Friday.

00:07:40.934 --> 00:07:43.968
Winston Duke joins us next.
Stick around, everybody.

